Maximum Flow – Edmonds-Karp Algorithm

This is a classical Network Flow problem.

It is the optimized version of Ford-Fulkerson using BFS.

✅ Problem Statement

You are given a directed graph with:

n nodes

Each edge has a capacity

You are given:

Source node s

Sink node t

Find the maximum possible flow from source to sink.

🧩 Example

Graph:

        (10)
   S ----------> A
   |             |
 (5)|           (15)
   |             |
   v             v
   B ----------> T
        (10)


Edges:

S → A (10)
S → B (5)
A → T (15)
B → T (10)

✅ Output

Maximum Flow = 15

Explanation:

10 units from S → A → T

5 units from S → B → T

Total = 15

🧠 Core Idea

We repeatedly:

Find a path from s → t

Send maximum possible flow through it

Reduce remaining capacity

Repeat until no path exists

❗ Why Edmonds-Karp?

Ford-Fulkerson can be slow.

Edmonds-Karp:

Always uses BFS

Finds shortest path in terms of edges

Time Complexity:

O(V * E²)

🔥 Key Concepts
1️⃣ Residual Graph

If we send flow:

Decrease forward edge capacity

Increase backward edge capacity

This helps "undo" flow later if needed.

2️⃣ Augmenting Path

A path from source to sink where:

All edges have capacity > 0

🪜 Algorithm Steps

Initialize maxFlow = 0

While BFS finds path:

Find minimum edge capacity (bottleneck)

Update residual graph

Add to maxFlow

Return maxFlow

☕ Java Implementation

We’ll use adjacency matrix for simplicity.

🔹 Step 1: Main Function
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public int edmondsKarp(int[][] capacity, int source, int sink) {

        int n = capacity.length;
        int[][] residual = new int[n][n];

        // Copy capacity into residual graph
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                residual[i][j] = capacity[i][j];
            }
        }

        int maxFlow = 0;
        int[] parent = new int[n];

Explanation

capacity[][] → original graph

residual[][] → modified during execution

parent[] → store BFS path

🔹 Step 2: BFS Function
        while (bfs(residual, source, sink, parent)) {

            int pathFlow = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

            // Find bottleneck
            for (int v = sink; v != source; v = parent[v]) {
                int u = parent[v];
                pathFlow = Math.min(pathFlow, residual[u][v]);
            }

            // Update residual capacities
            for (int v = sink; v != source; v = parent[v]) {
                int u = parent[v];
                residual[u][v] -= pathFlow;
                residual[v][u] += pathFlow;
            }

            maxFlow += pathFlow;
        }

        return maxFlow;
    }

🔍 Line-by-Line Explanation
While BFS finds path
bfs(residual, source, sink, parent)


If reachable → continue.

Find Bottleneck

Traverse backward from sink:

pathFlow = minimum capacity on path


That’s max we can send this round.

Update Residual Graph

Forward edge:

residual[u][v] -= pathFlow


Backward edge:

residual[v][u] += pathFlow


Very important for correctness.

Add to maxFlow
maxFlow += pathFlow


Repeat.

🔹 Step 3: BFS Implementation
    private boolean bfs(int[][] residual, int source, int sink, int[] parent) {

        int n = residual.length;
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.add(source);
        visited[source] = true;
        parent[source] = -1;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

            int u = queue.poll();

            for (int v = 0; v < n; v++) {

                if (!visited[v] && residual[u][v] > 0) {
                    queue.add(v);
                    parent[v] = u;
                    visited[v] = true;

                    if (v == sink)
                        return true;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}

